A rolled-back deployment lost the cost panel it had before the rollback
========================================================================
Date : 2026-07-29
Mode : unit + live (built dashboard in local mode over a real JSONL store
       written by the real recorder, page fetched over HTTP) + tsc/next build
Gate : make check 1520 -> 1528 (+8)

Found by : scripts/find_unwritten_keys.py (new) — the reverse-direction sweep.
           The forward sweep (find_unconsumed_fields.py) cannot see this class:
           the field is declared, populated and read. It is one of three writers
           that stays silent.


What was wrong
----------------
Three functions write an ACTIVITY row. Only one of them wrote `cost_metrics`.

  deploy_recorder._write_row            deployment_id=yes  cost_metrics=yes
  deploy_recorder.record_rollback       deployment_id=yes  cost_metrics=NO
  deploy_recorder.record_route_activity deployment_id=no   cost_metrics=no
  activity_writer.record_agent_activity deployment_id=no   cost_metrics=no

`record_rollback` writes its ACTIVITY row under the *same* deployment_id as the
deploy it supersedes, and it already had `steps` in hand — the exact input
`_cost_metrics` consumes. It simply did not call it.

That alone would under-report. What made it erase is the read side. In
dashboard/src/lib/activity-data.ts, `mergeActivity` folds every ACTIVITY row
sharing a deployment_id into one object:

    matches.sort(oldest first)          // deploy -> rollback
    const latest = matches[matches.length - 1]
    const mergedTrace = matches.flatMap(a => a.trace)
    return { ...latest, trace: mergedTrace }

The trace is unioned; everything else comes from the newest row. The newest row
after a rollback is the rollback — the one without `cost_metrics`. The detail
panel is conditional (`activity?.cost_metrics && ...`), so nothing threw and
nothing rendered "0". The panel was simply absent, directly above a trace that
had just grown to list both runs' tool calls.

Net effect: the moment a deployment was rolled back, its tool-call, reasoning
and token counts disappeared from the page — and the page showed *more*
activity than before, which is what makes the absence unremarkable.


BEFORE / AFTER (live, same fixture, built dashboard over HTTP)
---------------------------------------------------------------
Fixture: real recorder writes a deploy (3 tools, 1 reasoning entry, 800 in /
120 out tokens) then a rollback (2 tools) under the same deployment_id, into a
JSONL store the dashboard reads in DASHBOARD_DATA_SOURCE=local mode.

  BEFORE (both halves reverted, rebuilt, GET /deployments/DEP-D7D274DD -> 200)
    ACTIVITY rows        : 2   (cost_metrics present, ABSENT)
    trace tool names on page : 10 occurrences
    cost panel           : NOT RENDERED

  AFTER  (GET /deployments/DEP-71281D41 -> 200)
    ACTIVITY rows        : 2   (cost_metrics present, present)
    cost panel           : "tool calls 5  reasoning 1  tokens 920 (800 in / 120 out)"
    breakdown            : rollback_service x1  validate_deployment x1
                           build_image x1  push_image x1  deploy_service x1

The breakdown spans both runs, matching the merged trace beside it. Before the
fix the two panels described different sets of rows; that was the actual defect,
and fixing only the writer would have swapped one inconsistency for another
(the rollback's 2 tools reported as the deployment's, under a 6-entry trace).


The fix
---------
1. WRITER — src/agents/ai/deploy_recorder.py:record_rollback
   Attaches `_cost_metrics(steps, None)`, the same call `_write_row` makes.

2. READER — dashboard/src/lib/activity-data.ts:mergeActivity
   New `sumCostMetrics(matches)` sums totals and merges the per-tool breakdown
   across every row folded into the view, and `mergeActivity` sets the field
   explicitly instead of inheriting it through the spread. Sub-metrics now
   follow the same rule as the trace: they describe the whole lifecycle of one
   deployment id.

Deliberately NOT changed: `record_route_activity` and `record_agent_activity`
also omit `cost_metrics`. Neither writes a deployment_id, so neither can reach
the only view that renders the field — adding it there would manufacture
exactly the unconsumed field this sweep exists to find. `record_route_activity`
also omits `model`; the timeline falls back to `modelIdFromAgent(agent)`, which
returns null for "Supervisor" and renders a neutral mark, so absence is shown
honestly rather than guessed.


Falsification (each revert applied alone; restored green after every one)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
  1. record_rollback omits cost_metrics ............ 4 failed / 4 passed
  2. mergeActivity drops the explicit override ..... 2 failed / 6 passed
  3. aggregate over `latest` instead of `matches` .. 1 failed / 7 passed
  4. sumCostMetrics picks first row instead of sum . 1 failed / 7 passed
  restored ......................................... 8 passed

Guards (tests/test_rollback_cost_metrics.py, 8 tests):
  * behavioural — the rollback row carries the sub-metrics; a rollback with no
    steps reports zeros rather than absence; the summed total across both rows
    equals the tool entries in the merged trace.
  * derived, repo-wide — every ACTIVITY row literal in src/agents that carries a
    `deployment_id` must carry `cost_metrics`. `deployment_id` is precisely what
    routes a row into mergeActivity and onto the detail page, so the key, not
    the module, is what creates the obligation. Rows without it are correctly
    exempt. A new writer that carries one fails here. Plus a guard-the-guard
    assertion, because an AST walk that matches nothing passes vacuously.
  * structural on the TS (no TS runner in this repo — same approach the incident
    projection guard uses): the merge must set cost_metrics explicitly, derive
    it from `matches` rather than `latest`, and sum rather than pick.


New sweep tool
----------------
scripts/find_unwritten_keys.py walks the store boundary in the direction the
existing sweep cannot: keys the dashboard reads off an untyped record that no
writer in src/ produces. Writers are found by AST (dict literals *and*
`item["k"] = v` subscript assignments — the form the recent incident fixes used;
without it the tool reported the already-fixed `triggered_at`/`resolved_at` as
unproduced, which would have made it untrustworthy on first run). Vendored
node_modules under src/stacks are excluded.

Current output: 9 candidates out of 48 keys read off untyped records. All nine
are the documented nested-literal false positive — a dict built by a helper
(`reconciliation.to_dict`, `_cost_metrics`) and embedded by its caller, so the
helper itself does not write. That noise was left in rather than filtered: it is
what surfaced this defect, because the keys were nested *and* one of three
writers omitted the whole block.

The opposite direction ("written but nobody reads") is deliberately not
reported — the forward sweep does it at field granularity, whereas on raw dict
keys it drowns in k8s manifests, HTTP headers and CDK config.


Why the suite was green
-------------------------
`test_record_deploy_attaches_cost_metrics` asserts the writer that works. There
was no equivalent for the rollback writer, and no test at all over the merge
rule that turned an omission into an erasure. Both halves were individually
defensible; only the two together produced the failure, and only on the page.
